@rem Exercises for lesson 04_exam_tips (C track). Format: docs/CONTENT_FORMAT.md

@exercise c-exam-choice-compile
@type choice
@lesson 04_exam_tips
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Your file has a compile error, so the tests cannot run. What is the best thing to do?
@choices
- Submit it anyway and hope for the best
- Delete the tests at the bottom
* Fix the compile errors and compile again before submitting
- Rewrite every function from scratch
@explain
Tests can only run on a file that builds. Compile before you submit, read the first error message, fix it and compile again. A wrong result, by contrast, affects only that function.

@exercise c-exam-choice-wrong
@type choice
@lesson 04_exam_tips
@difficulty 1
@prompt
One function compiles and finishes but returns a wrong result. What happens?
@choices
* Only that function fails
- Every function in the file fails
- The file is not marked
- All functions after it fail
@explain
A wrong result fails only that function.

@exercise c-exam-choice-unfinished
@type choice
@lesson 04_exam_tips
@difficulty 1
@prompt
You cannot finish a function in time. What is the safest thing to do?
@choices
- Leave the half-written loop in the file
- Delete the function from the file
* Make it compile and terminate with a placeholder return value
- Comment out the tests at the bottom
@explain
A function that compiles and ends can at worst fail by itself. Half a loop can break the build or never end, and then the other functions cannot be tested.

@exercise c-exam-choice-not-fatal
@type choice
@lesson 04_exam_tips
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Which of these keeps your other functions from being tested?
@choices
- One function returns a wrong result
- Two functions return wrong results
* A loop in one function that never ends
- A function that returns the right value but is slow to write
@explain
A wrong result fails only that function. A loop that never ends stops the whole program, so the functions after it cannot be tested. Make sure every loop reaches its exit.

@exercise c-exam-theory-return
@type theory
@lesson 04_exam_tips
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Which statement should every function that returns a value reach before its closing brace, even a function you have not finished?
@answer
return | a return statement | return statement | return;
@hint
A placeholder such as return 0; keeps the file compiling.
@explain
A function that must return a value needs a return on every path. Write the placeholder return first and fill in the logic later.

@exercise c-exam-theory-loop
@type theory
@lesson 04_exam_tips
@difficulty 2
@prompt
A for loop is written as  for (int i = 0; i < 5; i--)  . What kind of error is this (name the problem in two or three words)?
@answer
infinite loop | an infinite loop | endless loop | an endless loop | infinite | endless | never ends | loop never ends | never-ending loop
@explain
i starts at 0 and gets smaller, so i < 5 stays true forever. An infinite loop keeps the tests from finishing, so check every loop before you submit.

@exercise c-exam-predict-countdown
@type predict
@lesson 04_exam_tips
@difficulty 1
@prompt
This loop ends because its variable moves towards the exit. What does it print?
@code
int i = 10;
while (i > 7) {
    printf("%d ", i);
    i--;
}
printf("\n");
@answer
10 9 8
@explain
i is printed while it is greater than 7: 10, 9 and 8. Before you submit, check for every loop that the variable moves towards the exit.

@exercise c-exam-fill-placeholder
@type fill
@lesson 04_exam_tips
@difficulty 1
@prompt
You have no time to finish sumTo. Make it compile and end by returning a placeholder value.
@code
int sumTo(int n) {
    // not finished yet: keep the file compiling and ending
    ___ 0;
}
@answer
return
@explain
A function that compiles and ends can at worst fail by itself. A half-written body can break the build or never end, and the other functions then cannot be tested.

@exercise c-exam-bug-loop
@type bug
@lesson 04_exam_tips
@difficulty 1
@prompt
This program should print 0 1 2 3 4, but it never ends. Click the buggy line, then type the corrected line.
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i--) {
        printf("%d ", i);
    }
    return 0;
}
@bugline 4
@answer
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
@explain
i-- moves away from the exit condition, so the loop never ends. The tests never finish, so no function can be checked.

@exercise c-exam-bug-semicolon
@type bug
@lesson 04_exam_tips
@difficulty 1
@prompt
This file does not compile. Click the buggy line, then type the corrected line.
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int square(int x) {
    int result = x * x
    return result;
}

int main() {
    printf("%d\n", square(4));
    return 0;
}
@bugline 4
@answer
int result = x * x;
@explain
A missing semicolon is a compile error, so the tests cannot run. Compile after every function.
